  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Overcast conditions the rest of today give way \nto mostly sunny and breezy conditions on Sunday. Temperatures \nslightly cooler than normal through this weekend.\n\nSkies have trended toward mostly cloudy/overcast conditions with \nmodest NW breezes, in response to a rather potent shortwave \ndisturbance aloft seen on satellite imagery circulating near \nMontreal. While currently dry, there are a number of light but non-\ntrace 1-hourly QPF amts being reported by METARs in northern New \nEngland; while dry weather generally prevails, a sprinkle or light \nshower are possible as this shortwave disturbance moves southward \nthrough Southern New England late this afternoon into early tonight. \nAny additional diurnal warming looks minimal today with the cloud \ncover and northerly breezes, in the upper 30s to the mid 40s! Early \ncloud cover and northwest breezes will keep temps from really \ndropping quickly, but decreasing clouds after midnight should bring \nlows down to the upper 20s to mid/upper 30s. \n\nWe do expect there to be more sunshine on Sunday compared to today, \nalthough low-level thermal profiles still are rather cool. This \nshould support better mixing with northwest gusts in the 20 to 25 \nmph range, which will add a bit of a chill despite sunny conditions. \nExpect highs in the mid to upper 40s, but it will feel closer to the \nmid-30s if you're doing any hikes in the higher terrain. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Prolonged period of dry and quiet weather, with\nmoderating temperatures next week, yielding mild afternoons and\ncool nights.\n\nAll guidance, including AI models, have large amplitude central US \nridge this weekend slowly advecting to the East Coast next week. \nHence, a prolong stretch of dry weather for SNE. Regarding \ntemperatures, chilly to begin the week Monday, but height rises \naloft will support warming temps and with surface high setting up \nacross the OH Valley to the Mid Atlantic region, this will place SNE \nin a warming WNW flow. Given this setup, wouldn't be surprised if \nforecast highs overachieve into the mid 60s in the Wed-Fri \ntimeframe! Thus, the temp theme next week will be cool highs in the \nupper 40s and lower 50s Monday, jumping into the 60s mid to late \nnext week. These temps are about 10-15 degs warmer than normal for \nmid Nov.\n\nDew pts in the 30s along with light winds, will result in a large \ndiurnal temp spread, with seasonably cool nights, as lows dip into \nthe 30s for most locations. The mild daytime temps likely linger \ninto Fri, before a dry cold front moves across the region Fri/Sat. \nEnsembles suggest not much of a post frontal airmass change, with \nhighs only cooling back to seasonal levels, with highs in the 50s. \nHence, mild days and cool nights will be the theme for much of next \nweek.",
